the village of trevone lies 30  kilometers from the front line in  ukraine's donbass region  in an oasis of tranquility in the middle  of a crisis zone  jeff henrichenko has lived here for  eight years  but the cottage is poorly insulated and  cold  after all this was supposed to be  temporary  the lachenko's home is elsewhere in  occupied territory  jeffen  was a protestant pastor  when pro-russian separatists appeared he  did not hide his pro-ukrainian views  in his sermons he often said that he  would gladly give a free ticket to  russia to anyone who didn't support  ukraine  you could be shot for saying things like  that at the time  they could take your life toss you into  a basement somewhere those were terrible  times  we weren't prepared and could hardly  believe what was happening  the lachenkos got the warning in time  they were able to flee and have been  living here in chivonne ever since  the village is situated between crimea  and the occupied donbass region  in an area some expect to be the first  target of any russian invasion  but after what we experienced there's no  escaping the subject of war the entire  country is at risk of attack not just  here  we head towards the front line  there are checkpoints on every road in  the area  and the closer we get to the demarcation  line with the separatist forces the more  frequent they become  a ceasefire has been in place since  2015.  the front line has not shifted but the  fighting continues  la nina is one of the last villages on  ukrainian controlled territory  soldiers are everywhere but life goes on  locals have been following the build-up  of russian troops on the border very  closely  the situation is terrifying  the ukrainian military's foremost  position is on the outskirts of the  village just in front of the  demilitarized zone  a footbridge leads to no man's land  it's off limits to soldiers and  reporters  but people cross it every day the  villagers of starmarvka  which lies right between the two fronts  every day svetlana a teacher crosses  over to her  she doesn't want to reveal her real name  for people here maintaining good  relations with both sides is key to  survival a village is often been caught  in the crossfire  i have a job so i go to school every day  living here is hard dangerous even very  dangerous  but stopping now and just running away  no i can't do that i just can't  there aren't any children at the school  right now  but that's not due to the fighting but  because of coronavirus measures  the children are doing remote learning  the teachers come to school for its  better internet connection  the school is just 500 meters from the  military post  we go to work every day and the children  normally go to school  but we never know what's going to happen  in five or ten minutes or the next night  we're always nervous always scared  [Music]  in giovonne jeffen leschenko has decided  to build a new bigger and better  insulated house  he wants to live there with his children  and grandchildren  the longer the conflict drags on  the less likely the family will ever go  home  lots of people can't decide what to do  even though russian troops might one day  stand on his doorstep here too  he doesn't believe he'll have to flee  again  [Music]  it's not 2014 anymore  people won't just throw their hands in  the air and let the attackers walk right  in this time  today people here are ready to defend  their land  eight years into the conflict the people  here have no choice but to live with  this situation  [Applause]  [Music]
 

